{"key":"ACCESS","record_id":"R0082","title":"LLHR: Low Latency and High Reliability CNN Distributed Inference for Resource-Constrained UAV Swarms","authors":["Marwan Dhuheir","Aiman Erbad","Sinan Sabeeh"],"year":2023,"venue":"arXiv preprint (arXiv:2305.15858v1 [cs.DC])","DOI":"DOI_NOT_FOUND","anchors":[{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":1,"section_or_figure":"I","excerpt":"All the data processing load for different requests can be handled locally within the swarm without resorting to remote servers."}]}
{"key":"ACCESS","record_id":"R0019","title":"Deep Reinforcement Learning for Trajectory Path Planning and Distributed Inference in Resource-Constrained UAV Swarms","authors":["Marwan Dhuheir","Emna Baccour","Aiman Erbad","Sinan Sabeeh Al-Obaidi","Mounir Hamdi"],"year":2023,"venue":"IEEE Internet of Things Journal, Vol. 10, No. 9, pp. 8185-8201, 1 May 2023","DOI":"10.1109/JIOT.2022.3231341","anchors":[{"record_id":"R0019","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0019.pdf","physical_page":1,"section_or_figure":"I","excerpt":"Some UAV applications require more than one device to accomplish their mission with high accuracy."}]}
{"key":"TOPOLOGY","finding":"Explicit evidence: R0082 models a peer-to-peer wireless ad hoc network of U UAVs operating at the same altitude over a v x q grid (coordinates (x_i, y_i)), executing CNN inference locally within the swarm via per-layer delegation across connected UAVs without remote server offloading (pp. 2, 4). Optimization is executed periodically over time to handle system dynamics (p. 4), and P2 enforces circular boundary and collision-avoidance separation constraints (d_{i,k} >= 2R, p. 4). Limited detail / not reported: R0082 does not specify whether the periodic P1-P3 solver executes centrally or in a decentralized manner, nor does it specify multi-hop routing beyond direct pairwise UAV-to-UAV links.","status":"YES","anchors":[{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":2,"section_or_figure":"II","excerpt":"In this approach, we assume that a group of UAVs, denoted by U, is capable to form a wireless ad hoc network."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":2,"section_or_figure":"II","excerpt":"The captured requests are divided into subtasks, and each UAV is dedicated to executing one subtask."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":4,"section_or_figure":"III.C","excerpt":"The collaboration of UAVs allows the system to accomplish the mission within the UAVs swarm without the need for transmitting it to remote servers."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":4,"section_or_figure":"III.C","excerpt":"We emphasize that we adopted the method of per-layer distribution as it is suitable for systems with a limited number of UAVs."}]}
{"key":"EVALUATION","finding":"Explicit evidence: Simulation-only evaluation in a 480m x 480m surveillance area (144 cells of 40m x 40m, R = 20m) using two CNNs—5-layer LeNet (32x32x3 RGB) and 8-layer AlexNet (227x227x3 RGB)—and three Raspberry Pi B+/3B+ device profiles (1.4 GHz quad-core, 1 GB RAM; e_i = 560, 512, 256 multiplications/s; labeled m = 560, 512, 256 in Fig. 3). Outcomes reported as curves across Figs. 2-5: Average Latency vs. Maximum Power for UAVs = 5, 7 and BW = 10, 20 MHz (Fig. 2: 'Fig. 2. Average Latency for different UAVs and Bandwidths and UAVs.'); Average Latency vs. Number of Requests for LeNet and AlexNet (Fig. 3: 'Fig. 3. Average Latency for 5-layers LeNet and 8-layers AlexNet'); Average Minimum Power vs. Available Bandwidth for LeNet (UAVs = 5, 9) and AlexNet (UAVs = 8, 10) (Fig. 4: 'Fig. 4. Average Minimum Power for 5-layers LeNet and 8-layers AlexNet in Different UAVs'); and Average Latency vs. Number of Requests comparing LLHR against a static-path Heuristic Solution and Random Selection Baseline solution (Fig. 5: 'Fig. 5. Average Latency for Different Requests in Different Models.'). Limited detail / not reported (pp. 5-6): no hardware testbed, no numeric summary values in prose, no simulator/solver software, no trial counts or variance/confidence intervals, no solver runtime, and no classification accuracy or image dataset.","status":"YES","anchors":[{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":5,"section_or_figure":"IV","excerpt":"We adopted an area of 480m x 480m to be covered by the UAV swarm, in which we have 144 equal size cells with 40m width and 40m length with radius R of 20m."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":5,"section_or_figure":"Fig. 2","excerpt":"Fig. 2. Average Latency for different UAVs and Bandwidths and UAVs."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":6,"section_or_figure":"Fig. 5","excerpt":"Fig. 5. Average Latency for Different Requests in Different Models."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":6,"section_or_figure":"IV","excerpt":"Figure 5 compares our LLHR approach with two other approaches, one is a heuristic solution, and the other is a random selection baseline approach as we vary the number of requests."}]}
{"key":"D01","finding":"Explicit evidence: The system question and mathematical model are clearly articulated across the Abstract and Sections I-III (pp. 1-5): minimizing end-to-end CNN classification latency and UAV transmit power while guaranteeing transmission reliability across resource-constrained moving UAVs via three sequential subproblems—P1 transmit power optimization (Eqs. 6-7), P2 UAV position optimization (Eqs. 8-9), and P3 per-layer allocation optimization (Eqs. 10-14)—with explicit layer computation/memory and channel/rate equations (Eqs. 1-5). Limited detail: minor notational/formulation inconsistencies appear (P1 is labeled MILP despite continuous power variables, P3 contains a bilinear product delta_{r,i,j}*delta_{r,k,j+1} while labeled ILP, and Fig. 3 labels e_i values as m).","status":"YES","anchors":[{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":1,"section_or_figure":"Abstract","excerpt":"Our system model deals with real-time requests, aiming to find the optimal transmission power that guarantees higher reliability and low latency."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":3,"section_or_figure":"III","excerpt":"We formulate this scenario into three sub-problems; the first one aims to find the optimal transmit power of the connected UAVS based on the positions of the distributed UAVs in the grid."}]}
{"key":"D03","finding":"Explicit evidence (partial configuration): Section IV (p. 5) specifies the 480m x 480m area (144 cells of 40m x 40m, R = 20m), LeNet (32x32x3) and AlexNet (227x227x3), three Raspberry Pi B+/3B+ profiles (1.4 GHz quad-core, 1 GB RAM; e_i = 560, 512, 256), thermal noise sigma^2 = -170 dBm, mean path gain h_0 = 10^-5, P_max = 120 mW, and packet transmission duration tau = 10^-4, plus bandwidths (10/20 MHz and Fig. 4 sweep) and UAV counts (5, 7, 8, 9, 10). Not reported (scope examined: full paper pp. 1-6): simulator/solver software, versions, and tolerances (p. 4 mentions only generic convex optimization with exhaustive search); per-layer c_j, m_j, K_j, K_s, and bit-width b; per-UAV memory/compute limits; request arrival distribution; optimization period length; unit of tau; random seeds/trial counts; exact static path definition for the Heuristic baseline; and which CNN/UAV count/bandwidth is used in Fig. 5.","status":"PARTIAL","anchors":[{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":4,"section_or_figure":"III.A","excerpt":"a combination of traditional convex optimization algorithms with simple algorithms like exhaustive search can be used to obtain the optimal solution when the optimization limits are set properly."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":5,"section_or_figure":"IV","excerpt":"Particularly, all types are characterized by a 1.4 GHz 64-bit quad-core processor and 1 GB RAM, while they can perform a different number of multiplications per second"},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":6,"section_or_figure":"IV","excerpt":"In the heuristic solution, the system model configuration is the same as the LLHR model, except that the UAVs have a static path to follow that is defined in the input configuration."}]}
{"key":"D07","finding":"Explicit evidence: Mobility is modeled in 2D at uniform altitude (coordinates (x_i, y_i) over a v x q grid, p. 2) with periodic position optimization P2 subject to circular boundary x_i^2 + y_i^2 <= R^2 and collision-avoidance separation d_{i,k} >= 2R (p. 4), updating inter-UAV data rate rho_{i,k} with distance over time (p. 5). Channel is modeled as Line of Sight (LoS) with Non-LoS explicitly ignored (p. 3), path gain h_{i,k} = h_0 / d(i,k)^2, and Shannon lower-bound rate rho_{i,k}(lb) = B_{i,k} log_2(1 + P_{i,k}/sigma^2) (Eqs. 4-5, p. 3). Limited detail / not reported (scope examined: pp. 1-6): NLoS propagation and interference under channel modes are explicitly deferred to future work (p. 6); Eq. (5) contains only thermal noise sigma^2 without an explicit interference term; 3D altitude, UAV kinematics/velocity, propulsion energy, fading/shadowing, and Doppler shift are not modeled.","status":"PARTIAL","anchors":[{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":3,"section_or_figure":"II.B","excerpt":"Due to the direct link between UAVs, we assume that the transmission of data between UAVs meets the Line of Sight (LoS) model and ignore the Non LoS (NLOS) in this model."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":4,"section_or_figure":"III.B","excerpt":"the constraint in equation (8d) is set to indicate the minimum distance between UAVs in order to avoid collisions between UAVs and make sure that UAVs are flying in a safe enough distance from each other."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":6,"section_or_figure":"V","excerpt":"In our future work, we plan to study the effect of interference on transmission latency when controlling channel communication under different modes like a line of sight and a non-line of sight."}]}
{"key":"D08","finding":"Explicit evidence: R0082 evaluates two CNNs of different depths (5-layer LeNet, 8-layer AlexNet), three Raspberry Pi B+/3B+ computational profiles (e_i = 560, 512, 256), and sweeps over UAV counts (5, 7, 8, 9, 10), bandwidths, maximum transmit power, and request counts (R = 1..20), stating compatibility with typical sequential-layer CNN pipelines (pp. 2-3, 5-6). Limited detail / scope constraints: evaluation is limited to sequential CNNs (no residual/branching or transformer architectures), a single 2D constant-altitude LoS grid simulation (480m x 480m), small swarm sizes (<=10 UAVs), no real image dataset or hardware testbed, and no UAV failure/churn or NLoS stress testing.","status":"PARTIAL","anchors":[{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":2,"section_or_figure":"I","excerpt":"We evaluate our system model through extensive simulations to prove its performance by considering different CNN networks, different device configurations, and different numbers of participants."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":3,"section_or_figure":"II.A","excerpt":"The distributed system is compatible with the typical CNN networks designed for a pipeline of successive layers (e.g., convolutional and ReLU)"},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":5,"section_or_figure":"IV","excerpt":"In the experiments, we used two different CNNs, a small CNN, namely LeNet (2 convolutional layers and 3 fully connected layers)"}]}
{"key":"D09","finding":"Not reported. Scope examined: entire R0082.pdf (physical pp. 1-6, including Abstract, Sections I-V, Acknowledgment, and References). No source code repository, simulation scripts, dataset link, or artifact availability statement is provided anywhere in the paper; the closing section contains only the NPRP grant acknowledgment and reference list.","status":"NOT_REPORTED","anchors":[{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":6,"section_or_figure":"ACKNOWLEDGMENT","excerpt":"This work was made possible by NPRP grant # NPRP13S-0205-200265 from the Qatar National Research Fund (a member of Qatar Foundation)."}]}
{"key":"FAMILY","finding":"Observable shared setup: Both R0082 and R0019 come from overlapping authors at Hamad Bin Khalifa University and Barzan Holdings QSTP LLC (Marwan Dhuheir, Aiman Erbad, Sinan Sabeeh / Sinan Sabeeh Al-Obaidi) under the same Qatar National Research Fund grant (NPRP13S-0205-200265; R0082 p. 6, R0019 p. 1) and address onboard per-layer CNN distributed inference in a surveillance UAV swarm. Both evaluate 5-layer LeNet (32x32x3 RGB) and 8-layer AlexNet (227x227x3 RGB) on three identical Raspberry Pi B+/3B+ profiles (1.4 GHz 64-bit quad-core, 1 GB RAM; e_i = 560, 512, 256; R0082 p. 5, R0019 p. 11), use the same three-term latency structure (t_s, t_i^(p), K_j/rho_{i,k}), plot latency over Number of Requests (R) = 1..20, and compare against static-path Heuristic and Random selection baselines (R0082 Fig. 5; R0019 Fig. 16). Observable differences in method and outcome comparisons: (1) Method: R0082 (§III, pp. 3-5) solves three mathematical optimization subproblems—P1 transmit power with reliability threshold (MILP/convex), P2 UAV positions with collision separation (QCQP), and P3 periodic per-layer allocation (ILP)—with no RL and no hot cells; R0019 (pp. 9-15) uses PPO2 deep reinforcement learning (MlpPolicy, 2x64 neurons) with hot-cell visiting QoS rewards (R_Omega, S_f) and an offline relaxed Sub-Optimal benchmark. (2) Setup parameters: R0082 uses a 480m x 480m grid (144 cells of 40m x 40m, R = 20m), variable power up to P_max = 120 mW, sigma^2 = -170 dBm, and BW = 10/20 MHz (p. 5), whereas R0019 uses a 100m x 100m grid (25 cells of 20m x 20m, plus 9/16/25/36-cell tests), fixed transmit power 0.1 W, sigma^2 = 7.9 x 10^-9, BW = 1 KHz, Poisson requests, and adds 16-layer VGG16 (p. 11, Table II). (3) Outcome comparisons and transcribed captions: R0082 Fig. 5 ('Fig. 5. Average Latency for Different Requests in Different Models.') compares 3 methods (LLHR Solution, Heuristic Solution, Random Selection Baseline solution) on Average Latency (s), alongside power-reliability plots (Figs. 2, 4); R0019 Fig. 16 ('Fig. 16. Total latency of different models.') compares 5 methods (Sub-Optimal solution, Our RL solution, OULD system model [19], Heuristic Solution, Random selection baseline) on Average Total Latency (s), alongside RL reward/penalty convergence (Figs. 4-7, 15, 18-19), constraint-meeting accuracy including PPO vs. Actor-Critic (Figs. 8, 17), shared data (Figs. 10, 12), minimum UAVs (Fig. 13), and hot-cell QoS vs. Linear/Sigmoid demand baselines (Fig. 14). Unresolved: Whether R0082 and R0019 share underlying simulation code, data, or experimental runs is unresolved because neither PDF documents code/run reuse or cites the other (both cite only their 2021 IWCMC paper).","status":"PARTIAL","anchors":[{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":3,"section_or_figure":"III","excerpt":"We formulate this scenario into three sub-problems; the first one aims to find the optimal transmit power of the connected UAVS based on the positions of the distributed UAVs in the grid."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":5,"section_or_figure":"IV","excerpt":"We adopted an area of 480m x 480m to be covered by the UAV swarm, in which we have 144 equal size cells with 40m width and 40m length with radius R of 20m."},{"record_id":"R0082","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0082.pdf","physical_page":6,"section_or_figure":"Fig. 5","excerpt":"Fig. 5. Average Latency for Different Requests in Different Models."},{"record_id":"R0019","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0019.pdf","physical_page":9,"section_or_figure":"Fig. 3","excerpt":"Fig. 3. RL system model."},{"record_id":"R0019","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0019.pdf","physical_page":11,"section_or_figure":"IV","excerpt":"Furthermore, we used three different types of UAVs that belong to the family of Raspberry pi B+ [45]."},{"record_id":"R0019","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0019.pdf","physical_page":14,"section_or_figure":"Fig. 16","excerpt":"Fig. 16. Total latency of different models."},{"record_id":"R0019","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0019.pdf","physical_page":14,"section_or_figure":"IV","excerpt":"Moreover, we compare our approach to two different baselines: 1) the greedy heuristic and 2) the random selection strategy."},{"record_id":"R0019","path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0019.pdf","physical_page":15,"section_or_figure":"V","excerpt":"Therefore, we introduced an online solution based on the RL algorithm that is possible for dynamic models and suitable for real-time applications."}]}
